WEBVTT

00:00:00.594 --> 00:00:01.470
Hello everyone.

00:00:01.643 --> 00:00:04.670
Today I want to talk about the tool you use most in this job.

00:00:04.995 --> 00:00:07.710
It is not the nippers, and it is not the brush.

00:00:07.928 --> 00:00:09.470
It is your own two hands.

00:00:09.726 --> 00:00:11.310
This lesson has two goals:

00:00:11.635 --> 00:00:15.360
to keep your hands from carrying germs from one client to the next,

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:17.390
and to keep those same hands healthy,

00:00:17.499 --> 00:00:19.710
so you can stay in this work for many years.

00:00:20.253 --> 00:00:22.346
Let me tell you about something very common.

00:00:22.748 --> 00:00:25.169
A technician puts on gloves in the morning,

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:27.706
and keeps them on from one client to the next.

00:00:27.984 --> 00:00:33.306
Answering the phone, opening the door, handling cash, still the same gloves.

00:00:33.561 --> 00:00:34.266
She thinks:

00:00:34.629 --> 00:00:36.906
I am wearing gloves, so I am clean.

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:41.386
But by now, those gloves are as dirty as an unwashed hand.

00:00:41.805 --> 00:00:43.146
Please remember this for me:

00:00:43.403 --> 00:00:45.626
gloves do not replace hand washing.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:50.186
Gloves are only an extra layer of protection, over hands that are already clean.

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:52.435
So when do you wash your hands?

00:00:52.683 --> 00:00:54.835
Before and after every client.

00:00:55.181 --> 00:00:58.035
Before putting on gloves, and after taking them off.

00:00:58.304 --> 00:01:03.155
After using the toilet, after eating, after touching rubbish or dirty tools.

00:01:03.529 --> 00:01:07.875
The guidance of the City of Helsinki, in Finland, says it very clearly:

00:01:08.146 --> 00:01:12.455
wash your hands with soap, then disinfect them, before every treatment,

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:14.195
and before putting on gloves.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.249
Before you wash, take a look at your hands.

00:01:17.433 --> 00:01:20.049
Rings, watches, bracelets:

00:01:20.319 --> 00:01:21.889
take them all off before work.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:26.209
Bacteria hide right underneath, where water and soap cannot reach.

00:01:26.555 --> 00:01:28.369
Rings also tear gloves easily.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:32.209
Your own nails, as a technician, should be short and clean.

00:01:32.555 --> 00:01:34.956
If you wear long nails, or enhancements,

00:01:34.906 --> 00:01:38.129
scrub under the nails really well every time you wash.

00:01:38.782 --> 00:01:40.222
Now, how to wash.

00:01:40.809 --> 00:01:43.662
The whole routine takes about forty seconds to a minute.

00:01:44.004 --> 00:01:47.742
Wet your hands, take some soap, and rub palm to palm.

00:01:48.172 --> 00:01:49.742
Then the backs of the hands.

00:01:49.925 --> 00:01:52.382
Interlace your fingers, to wash between them.

00:01:53.042 --> 00:01:55.422
Next, grip each thumb and rotate.

00:01:55.848 --> 00:01:58.142
The thumb is the place people forget most often.

00:01:58.402 --> 00:02:02.774
Then bunch your fingertips together, and rub them in circles in the other palm,

00:02:02.724 --> 00:02:05.182
to clean the fingertips and under the nails.

00:02:05.609 --> 00:02:08.302
Finally the wrists, then rinse thoroughly.

00:02:08.925 --> 00:02:11.031
Drying matters as much as washing,

00:02:10.981 --> 00:02:14.479
because wet hands spread far more bacteria than dry ones.

00:02:14.813 --> 00:02:17.759
Use a disposable paper towel, and dry thoroughly.

00:02:18.012 --> 00:02:20.871
Then use that same paper towel to turn off the tap,

00:02:20.821 --> 00:02:23.679
so your freshly washed hands do not touch it again.

00:02:24.016 --> 00:02:27.679
And never dry your hands on a cloth towel that the whole salon shares.

00:02:28.303 --> 00:02:30.242
Then comes hand disinfection.

00:02:30.748 --> 00:02:33.676
Take enough product to wet both hands completely,

00:02:33.626 --> 00:02:38.082
then rub the same way as when washing, until your hands are completely dry.

00:02:38.671 --> 00:02:43.235
That is usually about thirty seconds, but however long the product label says,

00:02:43.185 --> 00:02:44.162
do exactly that.

00:02:44.509 --> 00:02:45.202
And remember:

00:02:45.391 --> 00:02:48.402
if your hands are visibly dirty, wash them first.

00:02:48.693 --> 00:02:51.042
Hand sanitizer cannot remove dirt.

00:02:51.672 --> 00:02:53.361
Now let us talk about gloves.

00:02:53.589 --> 00:02:55.121
There are three common types.

00:02:55.375 --> 00:02:58.001
Latex gloves, that is natural rubber:

00:02:58.271 --> 00:03:02.875
they fit snugly, but they can cause allergies, for both technician and client,

00:03:02.825 --> 00:03:04.801
so many salons have dropped them.

00:03:05.209 --> 00:03:06.241
Vinyl gloves:

00:03:06.414 --> 00:03:11.281
cheap, but loose, they tear easily, and they protect poorly against chemicals.

00:03:11.667 --> 00:03:12.801
Nitrile gloves:

00:03:12.902 --> 00:03:16.321
stronger, better against chemicals, and free of latex.

00:03:16.652 --> 00:03:19.441
In our work, nitrile is usually the best choice.

00:03:20.051 --> 00:03:22.002
But here is something few people notice:

00:03:22.377 --> 00:03:24.802
gloves do not hold back chemicals forever.

00:03:25.147 --> 00:03:29.679
Gel, acrylic liquid and glue all slowly soak through a thin glove,

00:03:29.629 --> 00:03:32.802
within a few minutes to a few tens of minutes,

00:03:33.021 --> 00:03:34.802
even if the glove is not torn at all.

00:03:35.151 --> 00:03:37.122
Acetone gets through even faster.

00:03:37.471 --> 00:03:39.362
The Helsinki guidance also says:

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:43.602
disposable gloves only protect for a short time, so change them often.

00:03:43.761 --> 00:03:48.802
So, if a glove has gel on it, or is wet with acetone, change it right away.

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:50.482
Do not wipe it and carry on.

00:03:51.094 --> 00:03:52.721
So when do you change gloves?

00:03:52.977 --> 00:03:54.801
A fresh pair for every client.

00:03:55.058 --> 00:03:57.601
And whenever you touch something outside the work area:

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:00.801
your phone, a door handle, your own hair.

00:04:01.385 --> 00:04:04.241
The Helsinki guidance gives exactly this example:

00:04:04.562 --> 00:04:08.241
if you reach up to adjust the lamp during a treatment, you change your gloves.

00:04:08.661 --> 00:04:13.361
And disposable gloves are never washed, or sprayed with alcohol, to be used again.

00:04:14.016 --> 00:04:16.610
Taking gloves off also has to be done properly,

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:19.371
because the outside of the glove is the dirty side.

00:04:19.785 --> 00:04:24.031
First, pinch the outside of one glove at the wrist, and peel it off,

00:04:23.981 --> 00:04:25.451
so it turns inside out.

00:04:25.864 --> 00:04:30.171
Next, hold the glove you just removed in the hand that is still gloved.

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:35.594
Slide a bare finger inside the cuff of the other glove, touching only the inside,

00:04:35.544 --> 00:04:36.571
and peel it off,

00:04:36.759 --> 00:04:38.491
wrapping the first glove inside it.

00:04:38.727 --> 00:04:41.931
Put them in the bin, then wash or disinfect your hands.

00:04:42.231 --> 00:04:47.851
Because inside a glove, hands are warm and damp, and bacteria multiply very fast.

00:04:48.494 --> 00:04:50.418
About masks and eye protection.

00:04:50.909 --> 00:04:54.658
When you file and drill, dust rises right in front of your face.

00:04:55.159 --> 00:04:57.879
The most important thing is extraction at the table,

00:04:57.829 --> 00:05:00.498
because it removes the dust right where it is made.

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:04.898
An ordinary surgical mask only stops droplets and coarse dust.

00:05:05.298 --> 00:05:10.172
To stop fine dust, you need a particle filtering mask, such as an FFP2,

00:05:10.122 --> 00:05:11.938
fitted snugly to the face.

00:05:12.122 --> 00:05:15.378
Chemical vapors, though, are not stopped by a mask;

00:05:15.567 --> 00:05:16.898
for those you need ventilation.

00:05:17.154 --> 00:05:22.096
Safety glasses are a good idea when drilling, clipping nails, pouring acetone,

00:05:22.046 --> 00:05:23.538
or mixing disinfectant.

00:05:24.067 --> 00:05:26.029
Work clothes are protective wear too.

00:05:26.287 --> 00:05:28.029
According to the Helsinki guidance:

00:05:28.125 --> 00:05:32.108
smooth fabric, easy to wash, no thick, porous knits.

00:05:32.538 --> 00:05:33.469
Change them often;

00:05:33.726 --> 00:05:37.469
for services that can break the skin, a fresh set at least every day.

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:39.869
Hair tied back, short sleeves.

00:05:40.514 --> 00:05:44.520
Finally, something I want younger technicians to hear very carefully:

00:05:45.025 --> 00:05:47.000
look after the skin on your own hands.

00:05:47.429 --> 00:05:52.165
Lots of hand washing and long hours in gloves make the skin dry, red,

00:05:52.115 --> 00:05:53.320
and then cracked.

00:05:53.822 --> 00:05:56.040
Doctors call it irritant dermatitis.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:59.971
Once the skin is cracked, chemicals get in far more easily,

00:05:59.921 --> 00:06:02.200
and allergies often start right there.

00:06:02.467 --> 00:06:03.000
So:

00:06:03.115 --> 00:06:05.640
wash with lukewarm water, not hot.

00:06:05.747 --> 00:06:08.280
Dry your hands thoroughly before putting on gloves.

00:06:08.622 --> 00:06:11.960
Use a fragrance free hand cream on your breaks, and after work.

00:06:12.405 --> 00:06:14.546
When your hands are not visibly dirty,

00:06:14.496 --> 00:06:18.840
hand disinfection is usually gentler on the skin than constant soap and water.

00:06:19.189 --> 00:06:24.680
And if your skin stays red, itchy or cracked, and will not heal, see a doctor early.

00:06:25.357 --> 00:06:25.886
You see.

00:06:26.212 --> 00:06:29.326
Well washed hands, gloves changed at the right moment:

00:06:29.649 --> 00:06:31.246
clients will not notice most of it.

00:06:31.671 --> 00:06:34.846
But those are exactly the things that make a technician trustworthy.

00:06:35.188 --> 00:06:38.526
Rules differ from country to country, and from city to city.

00:06:38.868 --> 00:06:40.606
Ask the health authority where you work.

00:06:40.781 --> 00:06:43.486
And now, take the short quiz below the video.
